(NULL, 2, 'The Best Sandcastle', 'story', 'fiction', 'Leo and his big sister Ana were building a sandcastle at the beach. Leo wanted tall towers. Ana wanted a deep moat around the outside. They kept knocking each other''s work over without meaning to.

"Stop wrecking my tower!" Leo said.

"Stop digging in my wall!" Ana said back.

Their dad walked over and sat down in the sand. "What if the moat went around the towers instead of through them?" he asked. Leo and Ana looked at their messy sandcastle. They saw what he meant.

Ana dug the moat in a big circle, far from Leo''s towers. Leo built his towers close together in the middle, where Ana''s digging couldn''t reach them. Little by little, the castle grew — three towers in the middle, a wide moat around the edge, and a bridge made of a flat shell.

When the tide came in, water filled the moat just like a real castle. Leo and Ana stood back and looked at it together.

"That''s the best one we''ve ever built," Ana said.

"Because we built it together," Leo said.', 172, '[{"prompt":"What was the problem at the start of the story?","choices":["Leo and Ana had no sand","Leo and Ana kept wrecking each other''s work","It started to rain","They forgot to bring a shovel"],"answerIndex":1},{"prompt":"How did Leo and Ana solve their problem?","choices":["They built two separate castles","They stopped building","They gave the moat and towers their own space","Their dad built it for them"],"answerIndex":2}]'::jsonb, '[{"type":"summary","prompt":"In 2–3 sentences, tell what happened in the story.","starter":"This story is about","exemplar":"This story is about a brother and sister who keep wrecking each other''s sandcastle. Their dad gives them an idea to put the moat around the towers instead of through them. They work together and build their best sandcastle ever.","keywords":["leo","ana","sandcastle","moat","towers","together"]},{"type":"opinion","prompt":"Do you think Leo and Ana''s dad gave good advice? Write your opinion and one reason.","starter":"I think","exemplar":"I think their dad gave good advice because he helped them find a way to use both of their ideas instead of picking just one. That way nobody had to give up what they wanted to build.","keywords":["dad","advice","both","ideas","together"]}]'::jsonb),
(NULL, 2, 'Why Ice Melts', 'informational', 'science', 'Ice is water that got so cold it turned hard. If you leave an ice cube out on the counter, something strange happens — it slowly turns back into water. This is called melting.

Melting happens because of heat. The air in a room is warmer than ice. Heat always moves from something warm to something cold. So warm air keeps giving its heat to the cold ice cube, little by little.

Inside the ice, tiny pieces called molecules are packed together tightly, like a crowd of people standing very still. When the ice warms up, the molecules start to move faster and spread apart. Once they are moving and spread out enough, the ice isn''t solid anymore — it''s a liquid. That liquid is just water.

You can test this yourself. An ice cube left in a warm room melts fast. An ice cube left in the freezer stays frozen, because the freezer doesn''t send it any extra heat. The colder the place, the longer ice can stay ice.', 168, '[{"prompt":"According to the passage, what causes ice to melt?","choices":["Cold air","Heat moving into the ice","Wind","The color of the ice"],"answerIndex":1},{"prompt":"Why does an ice cube stay frozen in a freezer?","choices":["The freezer is too small","The freezer doesn''t give it extra heat","The freezer makes it colder than the counter","Ice cannot melt in a freezer at all"],"answerIndex":1}]'::jsonb, '[{"type":"summary","prompt":"Write 2–3 sentences telling how ice melts, using your own words.","starter":"Ice melts because","exemplar":"Ice melts because heat moves from the warm air into the cold ice. This makes the tiny molecules inside the ice move faster and spread out. Once that happens, the ice turns into liquid water.","keywords":["heat","melt","molecules","warm","liquid"]},{"type":"opinion","prompt":"Where do you think ice would melt fastest: on a kitchen counter or inside a freezer? Give your opinion and a reason from the passage.","starter":"I think ice would melt fastest","exemplar":"I think ice would melt fastest on a kitchen counter because the passage says heat moves from warm places into cold ice. A freezer is very cold, so it doesn''t give the ice any extra heat to melt it.","keywords":["counter","warm","freezer","heat","melt"]}]'::jsonb),
(NULL, 2, 'A Caterpillar''s Big Change', 'informational', 'science', 'A caterpillar does not look anything like the butterfly it will become. It is long, has many legs, and spends most of its time eating leaves. But hidden inside that little body is one of nature''s biggest surprises.

When a caterpillar has eaten enough, it finds a safe branch and forms a hard shell around itself called a chrysalis. From the outside, the chrysalis looks like it is just sitting still. But inside, something amazing is happening.

The caterpillar''s body is completely changing shape. New body parts are forming — wings, antennae, and thin legs made for landing on flowers instead of chewing leaves. This whole process is called metamorphosis, which means "a change in form."

After about two weeks, the chrysalis splits open. A butterfly slowly climbs out. At first its wings are wet and folded up small. The butterfly waits and pumps blood into its wings until they dry and stretch out flat. Then, for the first time, it flies away to look for flowers.', 175, '[{"prompt":"What happens inside the chrysalis?","choices":["The caterpillar goes to sleep and does nothing","The caterpillar''s body changes into a butterfly''s body","The caterpillar eats more leaves","Nothing happens until it hatches"],"answerIndex":1},{"prompt":"What is the word for the caterpillar''s whole change into a butterfly?","choices":["Hibernation","Migration","Metamorphosis","Pollination"],"answerIndex":2}]'::jsonb, '[{"type":"summary","prompt":"In 2–3 sentences, tell what happens to a caterpillar as it becomes a butterfly.","starter":"First the caterpillar","exemplar":"First the caterpillar eats leaves and grows. Then it forms a chrysalis, and inside, its body changes into a butterfly''s body. Finally, the butterfly comes out, dries its wings, and flies away.","keywords":["caterpillar","chrysalis","butterfly","wings","change"]},{"type":"opinion","prompt":"What part of a caterpillar''s change do you find the most surprising? Write your opinion and one reason.","starter":"The most surprising part is","exemplar":"The most surprising part is that the caterpillar''s whole body changes shape inside the chrysalis. I think it''s surprising because from the outside it just looks like it''s sitting still, but inside it is turning into something completely different.","keywords":["chrysalis","change","surprising","body","butterfly"]}]'::jsonb),
(NULL, 2, 'What Is a Mountain?', 'informational', 'geography', 'A mountain is a piece of land that rises up much higher than the ground around it. Some mountains are so tall that their tops are covered in snow all year long, even in summer, because the air gets colder the higher up you go.

Mountains form in different ways. Some are pushed up slowly when two giant pieces of the Earth''s crust bump into each other, taking millions of years. Others are made when a volcano erupts again and again, piling up layers of rock until a tall peak is formed.

Not every mountain looks the same. Some have sharp, rocky peaks. Others are round and covered in trees all the way to the top. The tallest mountain in the world is Mount Everest, and it is so high that climbers need to bring their own oxygen to breathe near the top.

People who live near mountains often use them in special ways — for hiking, skiing, or even farming on the lower, gentler slopes.', 164, '[{"prompt":"Why does it get colder near the top of a tall mountain?","choices":["Mountains are always in shade","The air gets colder the higher up you go","Snow makes mountains cold","Mountains are far from the sun"],"answerIndex":1},{"prompt":"What is one way mountains can form?","choices":["A river dries up","A volcano erupts again and again and piles up rock","The wind blows sand into a pile","A lake freezes solid"],"answerIndex":1}]'::jsonb, '[{"type":"summary","prompt":"In 2–3 sentences, tell what a mountain is and how it can form.","starter":"A mountain is","exemplar":"A mountain is land that rises up much higher than the ground around it. Mountains can form when two pieces of Earth''s crust push into each other, or when a volcano erupts again and again and piles up rock.","keywords":["mountain","rises","volcano","crust","form"]},{"type":"opinion","prompt":"Would you rather visit a mountain to hike in summer or ski in winter? Write your opinion and one reason.","starter":"I would rather","exemplar":"I would rather hike in summer because the passage says the lower slopes can be used for things like farming and walking, and it would be easier to explore without deep snow.","keywords":["hike","ski","mountain","summer","winter"]}]'::jsonb),
(NULL, 2, 'Deserts Are Not Empty', 'informational', 'geography', 'When people hear the word "desert," they often picture miles of empty sand and nothing else. But deserts are actually full of life — just life that has learned how to survive with very little water.

A desert is any place that gets very little rain all year. Some deserts are hot in the daytime and freezing cold at night, because sand does not hold heat the way soil does. Once the sun goes down, the heat disappears quickly.

Plants and animals in the desert have special tricks for staying alive. A cactus stores water inside its thick stem so it can survive for months without rain. Some desert foxes have huge ears that let heat escape their bodies, helping them stay cool. Many desert animals, like snakes and scorpions, sleep during the hot day and come out to hunt at night, when it''s cooler.

Not every desert is sandy, either. Some deserts are rocky, and a few are even cold, covered in ice instead of sand.', 170, '[{"prompt":"What makes a place a desert?","choices":["It is always sandy","It gets very little rain all year","It is always hot","No animals live there"],"answerIndex":1},{"prompt":"How does a cactus survive in the desert?","choices":["It grows very fast","It stores water inside its thick stem","It only grows at night","It has huge ears"],"answerIndex":1}]'::jsonb, '[{"type":"summary","prompt":"In 2–3 sentences, tell what makes a desert and how living things survive there.","starter":"A desert is","exemplar":"A desert is a place that gets very little rain all year. Plants and animals in the desert have special ways to survive, like a cactus storing water in its stem or animals coming out at night when it''s cooler.","keywords":["desert","rain","cactus","survive","night"]},{"type":"opinion","prompt":"Which desert survival trick from the passage do you think is the cleverest — the cactus, the fox''s ears, or animals sleeping in the day? Give your opinion and a reason.","starter":"I think the cleverest trick is","exemplar":"I think the cleverest trick is the cactus storing water in its stem, because it lets the whole plant survive for months without any rain at all, which seems like the hardest problem to solve.","keywords":["cactus","water","stem","survive","clever"]}]'::jsonb),
(NULL, 2, 'The First Airplane', 'informational', 'history', 'For a long time, people dreamed of flying like birds, but nobody had ever built a machine that could really do it. Two brothers named Orville and Wilbur Wright decided they would be the ones to figure it out.

The Wright brothers ran a small bicycle shop, and they used what they knew about building bicycles to help design their flying machine. They built a glider first and tested it again and again on windy hills, crashing many times but learning something new each time.

On December 17, 1903, at a place called Kitty Hawk, the brothers finally got their airplane, called the Flyer, off the ground. Orville flew it for just 12 seconds and traveled about 120 feet — shorter than a football field. It wasn''t very far, but it was the first time in history a machine had carried a person through the air under its own power.

News of the flight spread slowly at first, but the Wright brothers kept improving their design. Within just a few years, airplanes could fly for miles.', 166, '[{"prompt":"What did the Wright brothers do before they built their airplane?","choices":["They were pilots for other planes","They ran a bicycle shop and built gliders","They studied birds in a zoo","They worked at an airport"],"answerIndex":1},{"prompt":"What happened on December 17, 1903?","choices":["The brothers opened their bicycle shop","The first airplane flew with a person on board","An airplane flew across the ocean","The brothers gave up on flying"],"answerIndex":1}]'::jsonb, '[{"type":"summary","prompt":"In 2–3 sentences, tell what the Wright brothers did and why it was important.","starter":"The Wright brothers","exemplar":"The Wright brothers built and tested gliders before making their own airplane called the Flyer. On December 17, 1903, they flew it for the first time with a person on board, which was a huge first for history.","keywords":["wright brothers","flyer","1903","flight","first"]},{"type":"opinion","prompt":"Do you think crashing the glider many times helped the Wright brothers succeed? Write your opinion and one reason.","starter":"I think","exemplar":"I think crashing helped them succeed because each crash taught them something new about what didn''t work, so by the time they built the real airplane, they already knew a lot about how to fix problems.","keywords":["crash","learn","glider","practice","succeed"]}]'::jsonb),
(NULL, 2, 'How People Sent Letters Long Ago', 'informational', 'history', 'Today, sending a message to someone far away takes seconds. Long ago, it was very different. If you wanted to send a letter across the country, it might take weeks to arrive — and someone had to carry it the whole way.

Before cars, trains, or airplanes, people who delivered mail often rode horses. One famous group of riders was called the Pony Express. Riders would gallop as fast as they could to the next station, hand their mailbag to a new rider on a fresh horse, and that rider would race off toward the next stop. Riding this way, a letter could cross almost 2,000 miles in about ten days — which was amazingly fast for that time.

Later, trains made carrying mail even faster, since a train could travel through the night without stopping to rest, unlike a horse. Then came trucks and airplanes, which could carry thousands of letters at once.

Today we also have email, which can deliver a message in seconds. But long ago, ten days felt like lightning speed.', 169, '[{"prompt":"How did Pony Express riders make mail delivery faster?","choices":["They used trains","They handed the mailbag to a new rider on a fresh horse","They flew airplanes","They mailed letters by boat only"],"answerIndex":1},{"prompt":"About how long did it take the Pony Express to carry a letter across almost 2,000 miles?","choices":["One day","About ten days","One year","A few hours"],"answerIndex":1}]'::jsonb, '[{"type":"summary","prompt":"In 2–3 sentences, tell how mail delivery changed over time, based on the passage.","starter":"Long ago,","exemplar":"Long ago, riders on horses like the Pony Express carried mail across the country, taking about ten days. Later, trains, trucks, and airplanes made mail much faster, and today email can deliver a message in seconds.","keywords":["pony express","horse","train","mail","faster"]},{"type":"opinion","prompt":"Would you rather have been a Pony Express rider or send an email today? Write your opinion and a reason.","starter":"I would rather","exemplar":"I would rather send an email today because it only takes a few seconds, while a Pony Express rider had to ride fast on a horse for a long way, which sounds tiring and a little scary.","keywords":["email","pony express","fast","horse","rather"]}]'::jsonb),
(NULL, 2, 'Helpers in Our Community', 'informational', 'social-studies', 'A community is a group of people who live and work in the same area, and it takes many different helpers to keep it running smoothly. Each helper has a special job that keeps everyone safe and cared for.

Firefighters put out fires and rescue people and animals who are in danger. They also teach people how to stay safe at home, like checking that smoke alarms work.

Doctors and nurses help people who are sick or hurt feel better. They check on your body, give you medicine when you need it, and answer questions about staying healthy.

Teachers help children learn new things every day, from reading to math to how to be a good friend. Without teachers, it would be much harder to learn.

Mail carriers deliver letters and packages to every house, even in rain or snow. Trash collectors keep neighborhoods clean by taking away garbage every week.

Every one of these jobs matters. A community needs all of its helpers working together to be a safe, healthy place to live.', 165, '[{"prompt":"According to the passage, what is one job of a firefighter besides putting out fires?","choices":["Delivering mail","Teaching people how to stay safe at home","Giving out medicine","Collecting trash"],"answerIndex":1},{"prompt":"What is the main idea of this passage?","choices":["Firefighters are the most important helpers","A community needs many different helpers working together","Only doctors help people","Communities do not need helpers"],"answerIndex":1}]'::jsonb, '[{"type":"summary","prompt":"In 2–3 sentences, tell what community helpers do, using at least two examples from the passage.","starter":"Community helpers","exemplar":"Community helpers each have a special job that keeps people safe and healthy. For example, firefighters rescue people and teach fire safety, and doctors and nurses help people who are sick or hurt.","keywords":["firefighter","doctor","teacher","helper","community"]},{"type":"opinion","prompt":"Which community helper from the passage do you think has the most important job? Write your opinion and one reason.","starter":"I think the most important helper is","exemplar":"I think the most important helper is the doctor, because when someone is sick or hurt, a doctor can help make them feel better and stay healthy, which keeps the whole community safe.","keywords":["important","helper","doctor","firefighter","teacher"]}]'::jsonb),
(NULL, 2, 'Rules Keep Us Safe', 'informational', 'social-studies', 'At school, at home, and on the playground, there are rules everywhere. Sometimes it can feel like rules just tell you what you can''t do. But most rules have a very good reason behind them.

Think about a rule like "look both ways before crossing the street." This rule exists because cars are big and heavy, and a driver might not see you in time to stop. The rule keeps people from getting hurt.

Or think about classroom rules, like "raise your hand before talking." This rule helps everyone get a turn to share their ideas, instead of many people talking at once and nobody being able to hear.

Rules also help people trust each other. If everyone follows the rule of waiting in line, nobody has to worry about someone cutting ahead. That makes things fair for everyone.

Rules are not meant to make life boring. They are meant to keep people safe, help things run smoothly, and make sure everyone gets treated fairly.', 160, '[{"prompt":"Why does the passage say we have a rule to look both ways before crossing the street?","choices":["To make crossing slower","To keep people from getting hurt by cars","Because teachers like the rule","To make streets look nicer"],"answerIndex":1},{"prompt":"According to the passage, what is one reason rules are helpful?","choices":["They make life boring","They help things run smoothly and treat people fairly","They are only for school","They stop people from having fun forever"],"answerIndex":1}]'::jsonb, '[{"type":"summary","prompt":"In 2–3 sentences, tell why rules are important, using an example from the passage.","starter":"Rules are important because","exemplar":"Rules are important because they keep people safe and help things run smoothly. For example, looking both ways before crossing the street keeps people from getting hurt by cars.","keywords":["rules","safe","fair","cross","street"]},{"type":"opinion","prompt":"Pick one rule from the passage and explain in your own words why you think it''s a good rule.","starter":"I think the rule about","exemplar":"I think the rule about raising your hand before talking is a good rule because it helps everyone get a turn to share their ideas instead of everyone talking at once.","keywords":["rule","hand","turn","fair","good"]}]'::jsonb),
(NULL, 4, 'The Science Fair Surprise', 'story', 'fiction', 'Two days before the science fair, Priya''s volcano project stopped working. She had built it to erupt with a bubbling mix of baking soda and vinegar, but no matter how much she poured in, it barely fizzled.

"It worked perfectly last week," she told her older brother Dev, staring at the sad, dry model on the kitchen table. "Now it''s ruined."

Dev picked up the little plastic cup she''d glued inside the volcano to hold the mixture. "Did you check if this cup is sealed all the way? If the baking soda leaks out the bottom before it reacts, that could be your problem."

Priya hadn''t thought of that. She pulled the cup out and found a tiny crack along one side — probably from when the volcano had gotten knocked off the table last week. No wonder the reaction had gotten weaker each time she tested it.

She didn''t have another cup exactly like it, so she and Dev searched the recycling bin instead. They found a small yogurt container that fit almost perfectly inside the volcano''s opening. Priya sealed the edges carefully with hot glue this time, testing it twice to make sure nothing could leak.

That night, she mixed a fresh batch of baking soda and food coloring, ready for one final test. She poured in the vinegar, held her breath, and — the volcano erupted beautifully, red foam spilling down the sides exactly the way she''d planned.

"I really thought I''d have to give up," Priya admitted.

"You didn''t give up, though," Dev said. "You figured out what was actually wrong instead of just trying the same thing again and hoping it would work."

At the science fair the next day, Priya''s volcano erupted right on schedule in front of the judges. She explained not just how the reaction worked, but also how she had found and fixed the leak — which turned out to impress the judges even more than the eruption itself.

She didn''t win first place. But she came home with a ribbon for "Best Use of the Scientific Process," and she was prouder of that ribbon than she expected to be.', 361, '[{"prompt":"What was the real reason Priya''s volcano stopped working well?","choices":["She used the wrong colors","A cup inside it had a crack and was leaking","She forgot to add vinegar","The judges changed the rules"],"answerIndex":1},{"prompt":"What does Dev''s advice show about how to solve a problem?","choices":["Just keep trying the exact same thing","Find out what is actually causing the problem first","Give up if something breaks","Ask someone else to fix it for you"],"answerIndex":1}]'::jsonb, '[{"type":"summary","prompt":"Write a short summary of the story in your own words. Include the problem, what Priya did about it, and how it turned out.","starter":"","exemplar":"Priya''s volcano stops erupting well before the science fair, and she thinks it''s ruined. Her brother Dev helps her realize a cracked cup inside it was leaking the mixture. They fix it with a container from the recycling bin, and Priya''s volcano works perfectly at the fair, earning her a ribbon for using the scientific process well.","keywords":["priya","volcano","crack","dev","leak","ribbon"]},{"type":"opinion","prompt":"Do you agree with Dev that figuring out what was actually wrong was more important than just trying again? Explain your opinion with a reason from the story.","starter":"","exemplar":"I agree with Dev, because if Priya had just kept mixing more baking soda and vinegar without checking the cup, the leak would have kept ruining every test. Finding the actual cause let her fix the real problem instead of wasting more materials.","keywords":["cause","leak","fix","problem","test"]}]'::jsonb),
(NULL, 4, 'The Trail to Cedar Peak', 'story', 'fiction', 'Marcus had begged to go on the hardest trail at camp, and now, halfway up the rocky path to Cedar Peak, he was starting to regret it. His legs burned, and the trail kept climbing steeper and steeper through the trees.

"Can we take a break?" he finally asked his hiking partner, a quiet girl named Sofia who barely seemed out of breath.

Sofia stopped and handed him her water bottle. "You should have brought more water," she said, not unkindly. "This trail always uses more than people expect."

Marcus felt embarrassed. He''d packed light because he wanted to move fast and prove he could keep up with the older campers. Now his water bottle was almost empty, and they still had over a mile to go.

"I didn''t want to look like I couldn''t handle it," Marcus admitted.

Sofia sat down on a flat rock. "Everyone struggles on this trail the first time. My brother told me it took him three tries before he finished it without stopping every ten minutes." She split her trail mix in half and handed some to Marcus. "Slower is fine. Getting to the top is the point, not getting there fast."

They kept walking, but slower this time, stopping whenever the trail leveled out to catch their breath. Sofia pointed out things Marcus had been too tired to notice before — a hawk circling far above the treetops, tiny purple flowers growing out of cracks in the rock, the way the trees got shorter and more twisted the higher they climbed.

By the time they reached the top, the sun was lower in the sky, painting the valley below in orange light. Marcus''s legs still ached, but he barely noticed anymore.

"I almost turned back an hour ago," he said.

"I know," Sofia said, smiling. "That''s usually when people are closest to the top and don''t realize it."

Marcus looked out over the valley and decided that next time, he''d pack more water — and he wouldn''t be afraid to ask for a break.', 372, '[{"prompt":"Why did Marcus not bring enough water for the hike?","choices":["He forgot his backpack completely","He packed light to try to move fast and keep up with older campers","There was no water available at camp","Sofia told him not to bring any"],"answerIndex":1},{"prompt":"What lesson does Marcus seem to learn by the end of the story?","choices":["Hiking is too hard and he should quit","It''s okay to go slower and ask for help instead of hiding a struggle","Sofia is a better hiker than him and always will be","He should never hike again without an adult"],"answerIndex":1}]'::jsonb, '[{"type":"summary","prompt":"Write a short summary of the story. Include Marcus''s problem, how Sofia helped, and how the hike ended.","starter":"","exemplar":"Marcus struggles on a hard trail because he didn''t pack enough water, having tried to move fast to prove himself. Sofia shares her water and trail mix and suggests they slow down instead of rushing. They reach the top together, and Marcus realizes it was okay to ask for help and go at a slower pace.","keywords":["marcus","sofia","trail","water","slow","top"]},{"type":"opinion","prompt":"Do you think Marcus was right to feel embarrassed about needing help? Explain your opinion using details from the story.","starter":"","exemplar":"I don''t think Marcus needed to feel embarrassed, because Sofia tells him everyone struggles on the trail the first time, even her brother. Asking for help and slowing down let Marcus actually finish the hike and notice things he would have missed otherwise.","keywords":["embarrassed","help","struggle","sofia","finish"]}]'::jsonb),
(NULL, 4, 'The Water Cycle Never Stops', 'informational', 'science', 'Right now, the water molecules in a puddle outside might be on their way to becoming part of a cloud, a river, or even the rain that falls next week. Water on Earth is constantly moving through a process called the water cycle, and it has been running, without stopping, for billions of years.

The cycle begins with evaporation. Heat from the sun warms up water in oceans, lakes, and rivers, turning some of that liquid water into an invisible gas called water vapor. This vapor rises into the sky, where the air is much colder.

As water vapor rises and cools, it goes through condensation — turning from a gas back into tiny liquid droplets. Billions of these tiny droplets clump together in the sky, forming the clouds we see every day.

Eventually, those droplets in a cloud grow too heavy to stay floating in the air. When that happens, they fall back down to Earth as precipitation — rain, snow, sleet, or hail, depending on the temperature.

Once precipitation reaches the ground, water takes several different paths. Some of it soaks straight into the soil, becoming groundwater that plants can use through their roots. Some flows over the surface as runoff, joining streams and rivers that eventually lead back to lakes and oceans. And some lands directly on lakes and oceans to begin with, skipping the trip through soil entirely.

No matter which path the water takes, it eventually ends up somewhere it can evaporate again — and the entire cycle starts over.

This cycle matters enormously for life on Earth. It''s the reason freshwater keeps arriving on land even though land itself doesn''t hold much water compared to the oceans. Without the water cycle constantly moving water from the ocean to the sky to the land and back again, most of Earth''s fresh water would stay locked in the ocean, and rivers, lakes, and rain would eventually run dry.

Scientists estimate that a single water molecule might spend around nine days in the atmosphere before falling back to Earth as precipitation — but it could spend thousands of years locked up as ice in a glacier before melting and rejoining the cycle. The water cycle doesn''t move at just one speed; some water races through in days, while other water waits ages for its turn.', 398, '[{"prompt":"What happens during condensation in the water cycle?","choices":["Liquid water turns into water vapor","Water vapor cools and turns into tiny liquid droplets","Rain falls to the ground","Water soaks into the soil"],"answerIndex":1},{"prompt":"According to the passage, why does the water cycle matter for life on Earth?","choices":["It keeps the ocean the same size","It keeps freshwater arriving on land even though land doesn''t hold much water","It makes clouds look interesting","It only matters for glaciers"],"answerIndex":1}]'::jsonb, '[{"type":"summary","prompt":"Write a summary of the water cycle in your own words. Include at least three of its stages, in order.","starter":"","exemplar":"The water cycle begins with evaporation, when the sun heats water and turns it into water vapor that rises into the sky. Through condensation, that vapor cools into tiny droplets that form clouds. When the droplets get heavy enough, they fall as precipitation, and the water on the ground eventually evaporates again to start the cycle over.","keywords":["evaporation","condensation","precipitation","cloud","cycle"]},{"type":"opinion","prompt":"The passage says some water spends only nine days in the atmosphere while other water can be frozen in a glacier for thousands of years. Which part of the water cycle do you find more interesting, and why?","starter":"","exemplar":"I find the glacier part more interesting, because it''s surprising that a water molecule could be frozen and stuck in one place for thousands of years before finally melting and rejoining a cycle that usually moves so quickly for other water.","keywords":["glacier","thousands of years","frozen","melt","cycle"]}]'::jsonb),
(NULL, 4, 'Rivers Shape the Land', 'informational', 'geography', 'A river might look peaceful from the riverbank, but over long periods of time, moving water is one of the most powerful forces that shapes the surface of the Earth. Given enough time — sometimes millions of years — a river can carve through solid rock.

Rivers shape land mainly through a process called erosion, where flowing water slowly wears away soil and rock and carries the loose pieces downstream. The faster a river flows, and the longer it flows in the same place, the more material it can carry away. This is exactly how the Grand Canyon formed: the Colorado River has been cutting into the same stretch of land for millions of years, carving a canyon that is, in places, over a mile deep.

Not all of a river''s work is about wearing land away, though. Rivers also build new land through a process called deposition. As a river carries sand, soil, and tiny rock particles downstream, it eventually slows down — often where it meets a lake or ocean. When the water slows, it can no longer carry as much material, so it drops what it''s carrying. Over time, this builds up new land called a delta, often shaped like a fan or a triangle spreading out from the river''s mouth.

Rivers also change their own path over time. A river rarely flows in a perfectly straight line; instead, it curves back and forth in bends called meanders. As a river erodes the outer edge of each bend while depositing material on the inner edge, the curves can shift location over hundreds of years, slowly reshaping the land the river flows through.

Understanding how rivers shape land isn''t just interesting — it''s useful. Farmers plant crops in the rich soil that rivers deposit near their banks. Cities are often built along rivers because the water and flat land nearby made travel and farming easier. And engineers who build bridges or levees need to understand how a river erodes its banks so they can build structures that will last.', 372, '[{"prompt":"What is erosion, according to the passage?","choices":["Water dropping sand to build new land","Flowing water wearing away soil and rock and carrying it downstream","A river curving back and forth","Water freezing into ice"],"answerIndex":1},{"prompt":"How does a river delta form?","choices":["A river erodes a canyon","A river slows down and drops the material it was carrying","A river freezes solid","A river flows in a perfectly straight line"],"answerIndex":1}]'::jsonb, '[{"type":"summary","prompt":"Write a summary of how rivers shape land. Include both erosion and deposition, using details from the passage.","starter":"","exemplar":"Rivers shape land in two main ways. Through erosion, flowing water wears away soil and rock and carries it downstream, which is how the Colorado River carved the Grand Canyon over millions of years. Through deposition, a river drops the material it''s carrying when it slows down, which builds new land like a delta.","keywords":["erosion","deposition","grand canyon","delta","river"]},{"type":"opinion","prompt":"The passage explains why cities and farms are often built along rivers. Do you think the benefits are worth the risks of living near a river that can erode land? Explain your opinion.","starter":"","exemplar":"I think the benefits are worth it, because the passage explains that rivers bring rich soil for farming and make travel easier, which are things a community needs. Engineers can also study how rivers erode their banks to build stronger bridges and levees that help protect against the risks.","keywords":["benefit","risk","erode","farm","engineer"]}]'::jsonb),
(NULL, 4, 'Climate Zones Around the World', 'informational', 'geography', 'If you traveled in a straight line from the equator all the way to the North Pole, you would pass through completely different climates, from thick jungles to frozen tundra, even though you never left the same longitude line. That''s because Earth is divided into climate zones, and where a place sits on the globe has a huge effect on its weather patterns.

Near the equator, in the tropical zone, the sun''s rays hit the Earth almost directly overhead all year long. This keeps temperatures warm and fairly steady year-round, and many tropical areas also get heavy rainfall, which is why rainforests like the Amazon grow there.

Moving farther from the equator, you reach the temperate zones, which cover much of the United States, Europe, and parts of Asia. Temperate zones experience four distinct seasons because the amount of direct sunlight they receive changes noticeably throughout the year, as Earth tilts on its axis during its orbit around the sun.

Even farther from the equator lie the polar zones, near the North and South Poles. Here, sunlight hits the Earth at a low angle, spreading its heat over a much larger area and making it far less intense. Winters are long and dark, and even summers stay cold. Some polar areas are covered in ice year-round.

Climate zones aren''t only about distance from the equator, though. Elevation matters too — mountains can have cold, snowy climates even near the equator, because temperatures drop as you climb higher, regardless of latitude. Ocean currents and wind patterns can also shift a region''s climate away from what its latitude alone would predict.

Understanding climate zones helps explain why certain plants, animals, and even types of houses are common in different parts of the world. A house built for a polar climate needs thick insulation to hold in heat, while a house in a tropical climate is often built to stay cool and let air flow through freely.', 366, '[{"prompt":"Why do temperate zones experience four distinct seasons?","choices":["They are always cold","The amount of direct sunlight they receive changes throughout the year as Earth tilts","They are located at the equator","They never receive rain"],"answerIndex":1},{"prompt":"According to the passage, besides distance from the equator, what else can affect a region''s climate?","choices":["The color of the soil","Elevation, ocean currents, and wind patterns","The number of people living there","The type of houses people build"],"answerIndex":1}]'::jsonb, '[{"type":"summary","prompt":"Write a summary describing the tropical, temperate, and polar climate zones, using details from the passage.","starter":"","exemplar":"Climate zones change based on distance from the equator. The tropical zone near the equator is warm year-round with heavy rainfall. Temperate zones, farther away, have four seasons because sunlight changes through the year. Polar zones, near the poles, get sunlight at a low angle and stay cold, with long, dark winters.","keywords":["tropical","temperate","polar","equator","seasons"]},{"type":"opinion","prompt":"Which climate zone from the passage would you most want to live in, and why? Use a detail from the passage to support your opinion.","starter":"","exemplar":"I would most want to live in the temperate zone, because the passage explains that it has four distinct seasons, which means I could experience warm summers and cold winters instead of one climate all year long.","keywords":["temperate","tropical","polar","season","climate"]}]'::jsonb),
(NULL, 4, 'The Wright Brothers Take Flight', 'informational', 'history', 'Before 1903, many inventors around the world had tried and failed to build a machine that could carry a person through the air under its own power. Most of those attempts ended the same way — a brief hop off the ground, followed by a crash. Two brothers from Dayton, Ohio, Orville and Wilbur Wright, approached the problem differently, and it made all the difference.

The Wright brothers ran a bicycle shop, which turned out to be surprisingly useful training. Building and repairing bicycles taught them about balance, lightweight materials, and how a moving vehicle could be controlled by its rider — all skills they would need for flight. Unlike many other inventors who focused mainly on building a powerful engine, the Wright brothers focused first on control. They believed a pilot needed to be able to steer and balance an aircraft in the air, the same way a bicycle rider balances and steers on the ground.

To test their ideas, the brothers built and flew several gliders — aircraft with no engine — at Kitty Hawk, North Carolina, a location they chose because of its strong, steady winds and soft, sandy ground to cushion crash landings. Through hundreds of glider flights, they developed a system of wires and movable wing sections that let a pilot tilt and steer the aircraft, a breakthrough called "wing warping."

Once they were confident in their control system, the brothers built a lightweight gasoline engine themselves, since no existing engine was light enough for their needs. On December 17, 1903, Orville piloted their powered aircraft, the Flyer, for 12 seconds and 120 feet. Later that same day, Wilbur flew it for 59 seconds and 852 feet — a massive improvement in just a few hours.

The Wright brothers'' achievement didn''t get much attention at first; many newspapers doubted the story or ignored it entirely. But the brothers kept improving their designs over the following years, and within a decade, airplanes were being used around the world. What began as twelve seconds over a sandy beach eventually reshaped how humans travel, trade, and even fight wars, changing the world permanently within a single generation.', 384, '[{"prompt":"What did the Wright brothers focus on that many other inventors did not?","choices":["Building the most powerful engine possible","Control — the ability to steer and balance the aircraft","Making the airplane look attractive","Flying at night"],"answerIndex":1},{"prompt":"Why did the Wright brothers build their own engine?","choices":["They wanted to save money","No existing engine was light enough for their needs","Engines were not allowed at Kitty Hawk","They had extra parts from their bicycle shop"],"answerIndex":1}]'::jsonb, '[{"type":"summary","prompt":"Write a summary of how the Wright brothers achieved powered flight, including at least three key details from the passage.","starter":"","exemplar":"The Wright brothers used their bicycle-building experience to focus on controlling an aircraft, not just powering one. They tested gliders at Kitty Hawk and developed wing warping to steer. After building their own lightweight engine, Orville flew the Flyer on December 17, 1903, and Wilbur flew even farther later that day.","keywords":["wright brothers","glider","kitty hawk","wing warping","1903","flyer"]},{"type":"opinion","prompt":"The passage says other inventors focused on powerful engines, while the Wright brothers focused on control first. Which approach do you think was smarter, and why?","starter":"","exemplar":"I think the Wright brothers'' approach was smarter, because a powerful engine wouldn''t help if the pilot couldn''t steer or balance the aircraft in the air. Their hundreds of glider flights let them solve the harder problem of control before adding an engine at all.","keywords":["control","engine","glider","steer","balance"]}]'::jsonb),
(NULL, 4, 'Lewis and Clark Explore the West', 'informational', 'history', 'In 1803, the United States made a deal called the Louisiana Purchase, buying a huge stretch of land from France that roughly doubled the size of the country overnight. There was just one problem: hardly anyone in the U.S. government actually knew what was out there. President Thomas Jefferson wanted that to change, so he chose two men, Meriwether Lewis and William Clark, to lead an expedition west to find out.

Their mission, known as the Corps of Discovery, had several goals. Jefferson wanted them to map the land, study the plants and animals they found, and search for a water route that might connect the Mississippi River all the way to the Pacific Ocean. He also asked them to make peaceful contact with the Native American nations whose homelands they would be traveling through.

The expedition set out in May 1804 with a crew of around thirty people, traveling up the Missouri River by boat. As winter approached, they built a fort and stayed among the Mandan and Hidatsa nations, where they met a Shoshone woman named Sacagawea. Sacagawea joined the expedition as a guide and translator, and her knowledge of the land and ability to communicate with Native nations they encountered turned out to be essential to the expedition''s survival, especially when the group needed to trade for horses to cross the Rocky Mountains.

The journey was extremely difficult. The crew faced freezing winters, dangerous river rapids, steep mountain passes, and long stretches with little food. Despite the hardships, they finally reached the Pacific Ocean in November 1805, more than a year and a half after setting out.

Lewis and Clark did not find the easy water route to the Pacific that Jefferson had hoped for — it turned out the Rocky Mountains made that impossible. But they returned in 1806 with detailed maps, journals describing hundreds of plant and animal species new to U.S. scientists, and firsthand knowledge of the land that would shape how the country expanded westward for decades afterward.', 378, '[{"prompt":"What was one of the main goals President Jefferson gave Lewis and Clark?","choices":["To build cities in the west","To map the land and search for a water route to the Pacific","To fight a war","To find gold"],"answerIndex":1},{"prompt":"Why was Sacagawea important to the expedition?","choices":["She built their boats","Her knowledge of the land and ability to communicate helped the expedition survive","She was the expedition''s cook","She stayed behind and did not travel with them"],"answerIndex":1}]'::jsonb, '[{"type":"summary","prompt":"Write a summary of the Lewis and Clark expedition, including its goals and how it turned out.","starter":"","exemplar":"After the Louisiana Purchase in 1803, President Jefferson sent Lewis and Clark to explore the new western land, map it, and search for a water route to the Pacific. Sacagawea joined them as a guide and translator. Despite harsh conditions, they reached the Pacific in 1805 and returned with detailed maps and new scientific knowledge, even though they never found an easy water route.","keywords":["lewis","clark","sacagawea","louisiana purchase","pacific","jefferson"]},{"type":"opinion","prompt":"The passage says the expedition faced freezing winters, dangerous rapids, and little food. Do you think the expedition''s results were worth those hardships? Explain your opinion.","starter":"","exemplar":"I think the results were worth the hardships, because the passage says they returned with detailed maps and knowledge of hundreds of new plant and animal species, which helped shape how the country expanded for decades afterward — information nobody in the U.S. had before.","keywords":["worth","hardship","maps","knowledge","expand"]}]'::jsonb),
(NULL, 4, 'How a Bill Becomes a Law', 'informational', 'social-studies', 'Every law in the United States starts out as an idea. But turning that idea into an actual law that everyone must follow is a long process, built with many steps on purpose, so that new laws get carefully checked before they take effect.

The process usually begins when a member of Congress introduces a bill — a written proposal for a new law. Anyone can suggest an idea for a bill, including regular citizens, but only a member of Congress can officially introduce it. Once introduced, the bill is assigned to a committee, a smaller group of lawmakers who focus on a specific topic, like education or transportation. The committee studies the bill closely, sometimes holding hearings where experts share information, and can change the bill''s wording before deciding whether to send it forward.

If the committee approves the bill, it goes to the full chamber — either the House of Representatives or the Senate, depending on where it started — for debate and a vote. If a majority votes yes, the bill moves to the other chamber, where it goes through committee review and a vote all over again. Both the House and the Senate must approve the exact same version of the bill before it can move forward.

Once both chambers agree, the bill goes to the President. The President can sign the bill, and it becomes law immediately. Alternatively, the President can veto the bill, rejecting it and sending it back to Congress with an explanation. Congress isn''t necessarily finished at that point, though — if two-thirds of both the House and the Senate vote to override the veto, the bill becomes law anyway, even without the President''s signature.

This process can take months or even years for a single bill, especially for topics where lawmakers strongly disagree. Some bills never make it past the committee stage at all. While it might seem slow, this careful, multi-step system exists on purpose — to make sure that new laws get debated from many angles before they affect millions of people''s lives.', 375, '[{"prompt":"What happens to a bill after it is introduced in Congress?","choices":["It automatically becomes a law","It is assigned to a committee that studies it closely","It goes straight to the President","It is thrown away"],"answerIndex":1},{"prompt":"According to the passage, what can Congress do if the President vetoes a bill?","choices":["Nothing — the bill is dead forever","Override the veto with a two-thirds vote in both chambers","Ask the Supreme Court to force the President to sign it","Rewrite the bill and skip Congress next time"],"answerIndex":1}]'::jsonb, '[{"type":"summary","prompt":"Write a summary explaining the steps a bill goes through to become a law, using details from the passage.","starter":"","exemplar":"A bill starts when a member of Congress introduces it, and it goes to a committee for study. If approved, the full chamber debates and votes, and then the other chamber does the same. Both chambers must agree before the bill goes to the President, who can sign it into law or veto it — though Congress can override a veto with a two-thirds vote.","keywords":["bill","committee","congress","president","veto","law"]},{"type":"opinion","prompt":"The passage says the law-making process can take months or years. Do you think that slow process is a good thing or a bad thing? Explain your opinion using a detail from the passage.","starter":"","exemplar":"I think the slow process is a good thing, because the passage explains it exists on purpose so laws get debated from many angles before they affect millions of people. Rushing a law without careful review could lead to mistakes that are hard to fix later.","keywords":["slow","careful","debate","review","purpose"]}]'::jsonb),
(NULL, 4, 'Many Cultures, One Community', 'informational', 'social-studies', 'Walk down a street in almost any big city, and you might hear several different languages, smell food from three different continents, and pass by buildings used for celebrations from many different traditions — all within a single block. Communities made up of people from many different backgrounds are sometimes called diverse communities, and they exist because people and their families have moved from place to place throughout history.

People move to a new community for many different reasons. Some are searching for better jobs or schools. Some are escaping unsafe conditions in the place they used to live. Others move to be closer to family members who already live somewhere new. Whatever the reason, when people move, they usually bring parts of their culture with them — foods, holidays, languages, music, and stories passed down through their families.

Over time, these different traditions don''t just exist side by side; they often blend and influence each other. A city might celebrate several different holiday traditions each year. A restaurant might combine cooking styles from two different cultures into something new. Schools might teach students phrases from several different languages spoken by their own classmates.

Living in a diverse community comes with real benefits. Learning about different traditions can help people understand perspectives beyond their own experience. Trying new foods, hearing new music, and celebrating unfamiliar holidays can make everyday life more interesting. Diverse communities also tend to bring a wider range of skills, ideas, and ways of solving problems, since people from different backgrounds often approach the same challenge in different ways.

Of course, bringing together people with different backgrounds and traditions can also come with challenges, like language barriers or misunderstandings between groups who aren''t familiar with each other''s customs. But many communities have found that respecting each other''s differences, staying curious instead of judgmental, and looking for common ground helps neighbors from very different backgrounds build a strong, connected community together.', 358, '[{"prompt":"According to the passage, what is one reason people move to a new community?","choices":["To avoid learning new things","To search for better jobs, schools, or safety","Because all communities are exactly the same","To stop celebrating holidays"],"answerIndex":1},{"prompt":"What does the passage say diverse communities can bring, besides different foods and traditions?","choices":["Fewer ideas overall","A wider range of skills and ways of solving problems","Only problems and no benefits","The same language for everyone"],"answerIndex":1}]'::jsonb, '[{"type":"summary","prompt":"Write a summary explaining what a diverse community is and both its benefits and challenges, using details from the passage.","starter":"","exemplar":"A diverse community is made up of people from many different backgrounds who bring their own foods, languages, and traditions when they move. This blending can bring benefits, like new perspectives and a wider range of skills, but it can also bring challenges, like language barriers, which communities can work through by respecting each other''s differences.","keywords":["diverse","culture","tradition","benefit","challenge","community"]},{"type":"opinion","prompt":"The passage lists both benefits and challenges of diverse communities. Which do you think matters more, and why? Use a detail from the passage in your answer.","starter":"","exemplar":"I think the benefits matter more, because the passage explains that diverse communities bring a wider range of skills and ideas, and people can work through challenges like language barriers by staying curious and respecting each other''s differences.","keywords":["benefit","challenge","respect","diverse","community"]}]'::jsonb);
